Notes:
* The Gothic letters {{lang|got|𐌴}}, {{lang|got|𐍉}}, transliterated {{transliteration|got|e}}, {{transliteration|got|o}}, were used only for long [[close-mid vowel]]s ({{IPA|/eː, oː/}}). The digraphs {{lang|got|𐌰𐌹}}, {{lang|got|𐌰𐌿}}, transliterated {{transliteration|got|ai}}, {{transliteration|got|au}}, were used for [[open-mid vowel]]s (short {{IPA|/ɛ, ɔ/}} and long {{IPA|/ɛː, ɔː/}}).
*The "normal environment of occurrence" refers to native words. In foreign words, these environments are often greatly disturbed. For example, the short sounds {{IPA|/ɛ/}} and {{IPA|/i/}} alternate in native words in a nearly [[Allophone|allophonic]] way, with {{IPA|/ɛ/}} occurring in native words only before the consonants {{IPA|/h/}}, {{IPA|/hʷ/}}, {{IPA|/r/}} while {{IPA|/i/}} occurs everywhere else (nevertheless, there are a few exceptions such as {{IPA|/i/}} before {{IPA|/r/}} in {{transliteration|got|hiri}}, {{IPA|/ɛ/}} consistently in the [[reduplication|reduplicating]] syllable of certain past-tense verbs regardless of the following consonant, which indicate that these sounds had become phonemicized). In foreign borrowings, however, {{IPA|/ɛ/}} and {{IPA|/i/}} occur freely in all environments, reflecting the corresponding vowel quality in the source language.
*Paradigmatic alterations can occur either intra-paradigm (between two different forms within a specific [[Linguistic paradigm|paradigm]]) or cross-paradigm (between the same form in two different paradigms of the same class).  Examples of intra-paradigm alternation are {{transliteration|got|gawi}} {{IPA|/ɡa.wi/}} {{gloss|district ({{abbr|nom.|nominative}})}} vs. {{transliteration|got|gáujis}} {{IPA|/ɡɔː.jis/}} {{gloss|district ({{abbr|gen.|genitive}})}}; {{transliteration|got|mawi}} {{IPA|/ma.wi/}} {{gloss|maiden ({{abbr|nom.|nominative}})}} vs. {{transliteration|got|máujōs}} {{IPA|/mɔː.joːs/}} {{gloss|maiden ({{abbr|gen.|genitive}})}}; {{transliteration|got|þiwi}} {{IPA|/θi.wi/}} {{gloss|maiden ({{abbr|nom.|nominative}})}} vs. {{transliteration|got|þiujōs}} {{IPA|/θiu.joːs/}} {{gloss|maiden ({{abbr|gen.|genitive}})}}; {{transliteration|got|taui}} {{IPA|/tɔː.i/}} {{gloss|deed ({{abbr|nom.|nominative}})}} vs. {{transliteration|got|tōjis}} {{IPA|/toː.jis/}} {{gloss|deed ({{abbr|gen.|genitive}})}}; {{transliteration|got|náus}} {{IPA|/nɔːs/}} {{gloss|corpse ({{abbr|nom.|nominative}})}} vs. {{transliteration|got|naweis}} {{IPA|/na.wiːs/}} {{gloss|corpses ({{abbr|nom.|nominative}})}}; {{transliteration|got|triu}} {{IPA|/triu/}}?? {{gloss|tree ({{abbr|nom.|nominative}})}} vs. {{transliteration|got|triwis}} {{IPA|/tri.wis/}} {{gloss|tree ({{abbr|gen.|genitive}})}}; {{transliteration|got|táujan}} {{IPA|/tɔː.jan/}} {{gloss|to do}} vs. {{transliteration|got|tawida}} {{IPA|/ta.wi.ða/}} {{gloss|I/he did}}; {{transliteration|got|stōjan}} {{IPA|/stoː.jan/}} {{gloss|to judge}} vs. {{transliteration|got|stauida}} {{IPA|/stɔː.i.ða/}} {{gloss|I/he judged}}.  Examples of cross-paradigm alternation are Class IV verbs {{transliteration|got|qiman}} {{IPA|/kʷiman/}} {{gloss|to come}} vs. {{transliteration|got|baíran}} {{IPA|/bɛran/}} {{gloss|to carry, to bear}}, {{transliteration|got|qumans}} {{IPA|/kʷumans/}} {{gloss|(having) come}} vs. {{transliteration|got|baúrans}} {{IPA|/bɔrans/}} {{gloss|(having) carried}}; Class VIIb verbs {{transliteration|got|lētan}} {{IPA|/leː.tan/}} {{gloss|to let}} vs. {{transliteration|got|saian}} {{IPA|/sɛː.an/}} {{gloss|to sow}} (note similar preterites {{transliteration|got|laílōt}} {{IPA|/lɛ.loːt/}} {{gloss|I/he let}}, {{transliteration|got|saísō}} {{IPA|/sɛ.soː/}} {{gloss|I/he sowed}}). A combination of intra- and cross-paradigm alternation occurs in Class V {{transliteration|got|sniwan}} {{IPA|/sni.wan/}} {{gloss|to hasten}} vs. {{transliteration|got|snáu}} {{IPA|/snɔː/}} {{gloss|I/he hastened}} (expected *{{transliteration|got|snaw}}, compare {{transliteration|got|qiman}} {{gloss|to come}}, {{transliteration|got|qam}} {{gloss|I/he came}}).
*The carefully maintained alternations between ''iu'' and ''iw'' suggest that {{lang|got|𐌹𐌿}} may have been something other than {{IPA|[iu]}}. Various possibilities have been suggested (for example, high central or high back unrounded vowels, such as {{IPA|[ɨ] [ʉ] [ɯ]}}); under these theories, the spelling of {{transliteration|got|iu}} is derived from the fact that the sound alternates with ''iw'' before a vowel, based on the similar alternations ''au'' and ''aw''. The most common theory, however, simply posits {{IPA|/iu/}} as the pronunciation of {{transliteration|got|iu}}.

The following diacritics, not used in the original writing system, are sometimes added to vowel letters in transliterations:
* The [[acute accent]] {{char|´}} may be added to the digraphs ''ai'', ''au'' (written {{lang|got|𐌰𐌹}}, {{lang|got|𐌰𐌿}} in Ufilas's alphabet) to indicate their etymological origin in [[Proto-Germanic language|Common Germanic]], following a system devised by [[Jacob Grimm]]:
** <big>{{transliteration|got|aí}}</big> is used for the sound derived from the Proto-Germanic short vowels *''e'' and *''i'' before {{IPA|/h/}} and {{IPA|/r/}}.
** <big>{{transliteration|got|ái}}</big> is used for the sound derived from the Proto-Germanic [[diphthong]] *''ai''. Some scholars assume this sound remained a diphthong in Gothic. However, Ulfilas was highly consistent in other spelling inventions, which makes it unlikely that he assigned two different sounds to the same digraph. Furthermore, he consistently used the digraph to represent Greek {{lang|grc|αι}}, which was then certainly a [[monophthong]]. A monophthongal value is accepted by [[Eduard Prokosch]] in his influential ''A Common Germanic Grammar''.{{sfn|Prokosch|1939|p=105}} It had earlier been accepted by [[Joseph Wright (linguist)|Joseph Wright]] but only in an appendix to his ''Grammar of the Gothic Language''.{{sfn|Wright|1910|p=362}}
** <big>{{transliteration|got|ai}}</big> is used for the sound derived from the Common Germanic long vowel *''ē'' before a vowel.
** <big>{{transliteration|got|áu}}</big> is used for the sound derived from Common Germanic diphthong *''au''. It cannot be related to a Greek digraph, since {{lang|grc|αυ}} then represented a sequence of a vowel and a spirant ([[fricative consonant|fricative]]) consonant, which Ulfilas transcribed as {{transliteration|got|aw}} in representing Greek words. Nevertheless, the argument based on simplicity is accepted by some influential scholars.{{sfn|Prokosch|1939|p=105}}{{sfn|Wright|1910 |p=362}}
*The [[Macron (diacritic)|macron]] {{char|¯}} may be added to the letters ''a'' and ''u'' (written {{lang|got|𐌰}}, {{lang|got|𐌿}} in Ufilas's alphabet) to represent originally long vowels ''ā'' {{IPA|/aː/}} and ''ū'' {{IPA|/uː/}}. (Long {{IPA|/iː/}} is transcribed as ''ei'', following the spelling {{lang|got|𐌴𐌹}} used in the native alphabet). Macrons are often also used in the case of ''ē'' and ''ō''; however, they are sometimes omitted since these vowels are always long.  Long ''ā'' occurs only before the consonants {{IPA|/h/}}, {{IPA|/hʷ/}} and represents [[Proto-Germanic]] nasalized {{IPA|/ãː(h)/}} < earlier {{IPA|/aŋ(h)/}}; non-nasal {{IPA|/aː/}} did not occur in Proto-Germanic. It is possible that the Gothic vowel still preserved the nasalization, or else that the nasalization was lost but the length distinction kept, as has happened with {{langx|lt|ą}}. Non-nasal {{IPA|/iː/}} and {{IPA|/uː/}} occurred in Proto-Germanic, however, and so long ''ei'' and ''ū'' occur in all contexts. Before {{IPA|/h/}} and {{IPA|/hʷ/}}, long ''ei'' and ''ū'' could stem from either non-nasal or nasal long vowels in Proto-Germanic; it is possible that the nasalization was still preserved in Gothic but not written.

