Notes:
*{{IPA|/hʷ/}} is written in the native alphabet with the single letter {{lang|got|𐍈}}. It is transliterated by the symbol {{angbr|[[hwair|ƕ]]}}, which is used only in transliterating Gothic.
*{{IPA|/kʷ/}} is written in the native alphabet with the single letter {{lang|got|𐌵}}. It is transliterated as {{angbr|q}}, with no following ''u''.
*{{IPA|/ɡʷ/}}, however, is written with two letters in the native alphabet, {{lang|got|𐌲𐍅}}, and hence is transliterated {{transliteration|got|gw}}. The lack of a single letter to represent this sound may result from its restricted distribution (only after {{IPA|/n/}}) and its rarity.
*{{IPA|/θ/}} is transliterated as {{transliteration|got|þ}}.
*Although the [[velar nasal]] {{IPA|[ŋ]}} functions as an [[allophone]] of {{IPA|/n/}} before {{IPA|/ɡ/}} and {{IPA|/k/}}, it is written in the native alphabet as {{lang|got|𐌲}} ({{transliteration|got|g}}), following the usage of the corresponding Greek letter [[gamma]]. The transliteration of {{IPA|[ŋ]}} as {{transliteration|got|g}} leads to ambiguity in the context of the sequence {{angbr|ggw}}: for example, {{transliteration|got|saggws}} {{IPA|[saŋɡʷs]}} {{gloss|song}} but {{transliteration|got|triggws}} {{IPA|[triɡɡʷs]}} {{gloss|faithful}} (compare English 'true').

==Phonology==
Gothic [[phonology]] is reconstructed based on data such as comparison with other [[Germanic languages]], analysis of the transcription of Greek and non-Greek terms and names in Gothic, and analysis of spelling alternations and variations in Gothic texts.

