* {{IPA|/a/}}, {{IPA|/i/}} and {{IPA|/u/}} can be either long or short.<ref>See also {{harvnb|Cercignani|1986}}.</ref> Gothic writing distinguishes between long and short vowels only for {{IPA|/i/}} by writing {{angbr|i}} for the short form and {{angbr|ei}} for the long (a [[Digraph (orthography)|digraph]] or ''false diphthong''), in an imitation of Greek usage ({{lang|grc|ει}} = {{IPA|/iː/}}). Single vowels are sometimes long where a historically present [[nasal stop|nasal consonant]] has been dropped in front of an {{IPA|/h/}} (a case of [[compensatory lengthening]]). Thus, the preterite of the verb {{transliteration|got|briggan}} {{IPA|[briŋɡan]}} {{gloss|to bring}} (Dutch {{lang|nl|brengen}}, German {{lang|de|bringen}}) becomes {{transliteration|got|brahta}} {{IPA|/braːhta/}} (English ''brought'', Dutch {{lang|nl|bracht}},  German {{lang|de|brachte}}), from [[Proto-Germanic]] *''branhtē''. In detailed [[transliteration]], when the intent is more [[phonetic transcription]], length is noted by a macron (or failing that, often a [[circumflex]]): {{transliteration|got|brāhta}}, {{transliteration|got|brâhta}}. This is the only context in which {{IPA|/aː/}} appears natively whereas {{IPA|/uː/}}, like {{IPA|/iː/}}, is found often enough in other contexts: {{transliteration|got|brūks}} {{gloss|useful}} (Dutch {{lang|nl|(ge)bruik}}, German {{lang|de|(Ge)brauch}}, Icelandic {{lang|is|brúk}} {{gloss|use}}).
* {{IPA|/eː/}} and {{IPA|/oː/}} are long [[close-mid vowel]]s. They are written as {{angbr|e}} and {{angbr|o}}: {{transliteration|got|neƕ}} {{IPA|[neːʍ]}} {{gloss|near}} (English ''nigh'', Dutch {{lang|nl|nader}}, German {{lang|de|nah}}); {{transliteration|got|fodjan}} {{IPA|[foːdjan]}} {{gloss|to feed}}.
* {{IPA|/ɛ/}} and {{IPA|/ɔ/}} are short [[open-mid vowel]]s.<ref>For the Gothic short vowels see also {{harvnb|Cercignani|1979b}}.</ref> They are noted using the digraphs ''ai'' and ''au'': {{transliteration|got|taihun}} {{IPA|[tɛhun]}} {{gloss|ten}} (Dutch {{lang|nl|tien}}, German {{lang|de|zehn}}, Icelandic {{lang|is|tíu}}), {{transliteration|got|dauhtar}} {{IPA|/dɔhtar/}} {{gloss|daughter}} (Dutch {{lang|nl|dochter}}, German {{lang|de|Tochter}}, Icelandic {{lang|is|dóttir}}). In transliterating Gothic, accents are placed on the second vowel of these digraphs ''aí'' and ''aú'' to distinguish them from the original diphthongs ''ái'' and ''áu'': {{transliteration|got|taíhun}}, {{transliteration|got|daúhtar}}. In most cases short {{IPA|[ɛ]}} and {{IPA|[ɔ]}} are allophones of {{IPA|/i, u/}} before {{IPA|/r, h, ʍ/}}.<ref>But see {{harvnb|Cercignani|1984}}.</ref> Furthermore, the reduplication syllable of the reduplicating preterites has ''ai'' as well, which was probably pronounced as a short {{IPA|[ɛ]}}.<ref>See also {{harvnb|Cercignani|1979a}}.</ref> Finally, short {{IPA|[ɛ]}} and {{IPA|[ɔ]}} occur in loan words from Greek and Latin ({{transliteration|got|aípiskaúpus}} {{IPA|[ɛpiskɔpus]}} = {{lang|grc|ἐπίσκοπος}} {{gloss|bishop}}, {{transliteration|got|laíktjo}} {{IPA|[lɛktjoː]}} = {{lang|lat|lectio}} {{gloss|lection}}, {{transliteration|got|Paúntius}} {{IPA|[pɔntius]}} = {{lang|lat|Pontius}}).
* The Germanic diphthongs {{IPA|/ai/}} and {{IPA|/au/}} appear as digraphs written {{angle bracket|ai}} and {{angle bracket|au}} in Gothic. Researchers have disagreed over whether they were still pronounced as diphthongs {{IPA|/ai̯/}} and {{IPA|/au̯/}} in Ulfilas's time (4th century) or had become long open-mid vowels: {{IPA|/ɛː/}} and {{IPA|/ɔː/}}: {{transliteration|got|ains}} {{IPA|[ains] / [ɛːns]}} {{gloss|one}} (German {{lang|de|eins}}, Icelandic {{lang|is|einn}}), {{transliteration|got|augo}} {{IPA|[auɣoː] / [ɔːɣoː]}} {{gloss|eye}} (German {{lang|de|Auge}}, Icelandic {{lang|is|auga}}). It is most likely that the latter view is correct, as it is indisputable that the digraphs {{angle bracket|ai}} and {{angle bracket|au}} represent the sounds {{IPA|/ɛː/}} and {{IPA|/ɔː/}} in some circumstances (see below), and {{angle bracket|aj}} and {{angle bracket|aw}} were available to unambiguously represent the sounds {{IPA|/ai̯/}} and {{IPA|/au̯/}}. The digraph {{angle bracket|aw}} is in fact used to represent {{IPA|/au/}} in foreign words (such as {{transliteration|got|Pawlus}} {{gloss|Paul}}), and alternations between {{angle bracket|ai}}/{{angle bracket|aj}} and {{angle bracket|au}}/{{angle bracket|aw}} are scrupulously maintained in paradigms where both variants occur (e.g. {{transliteration|got|taujan}} {{gloss|to do}} vs. past tense {{transliteration|got|tawida}} {{gloss|did}}). Evidence from transcriptions of Gothic names into Latin suggests that the sound change had occurred very recently when Gothic spelling was standardized: Gothic names with Germanic ''au'' are rendered with ''au'' in Latin until the 4th century and ''o'' later on ({{lang|lat|Austrogoti}} > {{lang|lat|Ostrogoti}}). The digraphs {{angle bracket|ai}} and {{angle bracket|au}} are normally written with an accent on the first vowel (''ái, áu'') when they correspond to Proto-Germanic {{IPA|/ai̯/}} and {{IPA|/au̯/}}.
* Long {{IPA|[ɛː]}} and {{IPA|[ɔː]}} also occur as allophones of {{IPA|/eː/}} and {{IPA|/uː, oː/}} respectively before a following vowel: {{transliteration|got|waian}} {{IPA|[wɛːan]}} {{gloss|to blow}} (Dutch {{lang|nl|waaien}}, German {{lang|de|wehen}}), {{transliteration|got|bauan}} {{IPA|[bɔːan]}} {{gloss|to build}} (Dutch {{lang|nl|bouwen}}, German {{lang|de|bauen}}; Icelandic {{lang|is|búa}} {{gloss|to live, reside}}), also in Greek words {{transliteration|got|Trauada}} {{gloss|[[Troad]]}} (Gk. {{lang|grc|Τρῳάς}}). In detailed transcription these are notated ''ai, au''.
* The existence of a vowel {{IPA|/y/}} in Gothic is unclear. It is derived from the use of {{lang|got|𐍅}} ({{transliteration|got|w}}) to transcribe Greek {{lang|grc|υ}} ({{transliteration|grc|y}}) or the diphthong {{lang|grc|οι}} ({{transliteration|grc|oi}}), both of which were pronounced {{IPA|[y]}} in the Greek of the time. {{transliteration|got|W}} is otherwise used to denote the consonant {{IPA|/w/}}). It may have been pronounced {{IPA|[i]}}.{{sfn|Miller|2019|p=33}}
* {{IPA|/iu/}} is usually reconstructed as a falling diphthong ({{IPA|[iu̯]}}: {{transliteration|got|diups}} {{IPA|[diu̯ps]}} {{gloss|deep}} (Dutch {{lang|nl|diep}}, German {{lang|de|tief}}, Icelandic {{lang|is|djúpur}}), though this has been disputed (see [[#Alphabet and transliteration|alphabet and transliteration]] section above).
* Greek diphthongs: In Ulfilas's era, all the diphthongs of Classical Greek had become simple vowels in speech (''[[monophthong]]ization''), except for {{lang|grc|αυ}} ({{transliteration|grc|au}}) and {{lang|grc|ευ}} ({{transliteration|grc|eu}}), which were probably pronounced {{IPA|[aβ]}} and {{IPA|[ɛβ]}} (they evolved into {{IPA|[av~af]}} and {{IPA|[ev~ef]}} in [[Modern Greek]].) In words borrowed from Greek, {{lang|grc|αυ}} and {{lang|grc|ευ}} are transcribed in extant Gothic manuscripts as {{transliteration|got|aw, aiw}}, probably pronounced {{IPA|[au̯, ɛu̯]}}: {{transliteration|got|Pawlus}} {{IPA|[pau̯lus]}} {{gloss|Paul}} (Gk. {{lang|grc|Παῦλος}}), {{transliteration|got|aíwaggelista}} {{IPA|[ɛwaŋɡeːlista]}} {{gloss|evangelist}} (Gk. {{lang|grc|εὐαγγελιστής}}, via the Latin {{lang|lat|evangelista}}).
* All vowels (including diphthongs) can be followed by a {{IPA|[w]}}, which was likely pronounced as the second element of a diphthong with roughly the sound of {{IPA|[u̯]}}. It seems likely that this is more of an instance of phonetic juxtaposition than of true diphthongs (such as, for example, the sound {{IPA|/aj/}} in the French word {{Lang|fr|paille}} {{gloss|straw}}, which is not the diphthong {{IPA|/ai̯/}} but rather a vowel followed by an [[approximant]]): {{transliteration|got|alew}} {{IPA|[aleːw]}} {{gloss|olive oil}} ( < Latin {{Lang|la|oleum}}), {{transliteration|got|snáiws}} {{IPA|[snɛːws]}} {{gloss|snow}}, {{transliteration|got|lasiws}} {{IPA|[lasiws]}} {{gloss|tired}} (English ''lazy'').


