Gothic distinguished single or short consonants from long or [[gemination|geminated]] consonants: the latter were written double, as in {{transliteration|got|atta}} {{IPA|[atːa]}} {{gloss|dad}}, {{transliteration|got|kunnan}} {{IPA|[kunːan]}} {{gloss|to know}} (Dutch ''kennen'', German ''kennen'', Icelandic ''kunna''). Gothic is rich in fricative consonants (although many of them may have been [[approximant]]s; it is hard to separate the two) originating from [[Grimm's law]] and [[Verner's law]]. Gothic retained [[Proto-Germanic language|Proto-Germanic]] *''z'' as {{IPA|/z/}}, unlike [[North Germanic languages]] and [[West Germanic languages]], which turned this sound into {{IPA|/r/}} through rhotacization. Voiced fricative consonants were [[final devoicing|devoiced]] at the ends of words.

====Stops====
* The voiceless stops {{IPA|/p/}}, {{IPA|/t/}} and {{IPA|/k/}} are regularly noted by {{angbr|p}}, {{angbr|t}} and {{angbr|k}} respectively: {{transliteration|got|paska}} {{IPA|[paska]}} {{gloss|Easter}} (from the Greek ''{{lang|grc|πάσχα}}''), {{transliteration|got|tuggo}} {{IPA|[tuŋɡoː]}} {{gloss|tongue}}, {{transliteration|got|kalbo}} {{IPA|[kalboː]}} {{gloss|calf}}.
* The letter {{angbr|''q''}} probably represented a [[labialized voiceless velar plosive|voiceless labiovelar stop]], {{IPA|/kʷ/}}, comparable to Latin {{angbr|qu}}: {{transliteration|got|qiman}} {{IPA|[kʷiman]}} {{gloss|to come}}. In later Germanic languages, this phoneme has become either a [[consonant cluster]] {{IPA|/kw/}} of a [[voiceless velar stop]] + a [[labio-velar approximant]] (English ''qu'') or a simple voiceless velar stop {{IPA|/k/}} (English ''c, k''). [[Proto-Germanic_language#Labiovelars|Proto-Germanic]] replaced {{IPA|/kʷu/}} with {{IPA|/ku/}} (a continuation of the Proto-Indo-European [[Boukólos rule]]) but this [[surface filter]] was no longer active by the time Ulfilas first wrote the [[Codex Argenteus]]. The sequence {{IPA|/kʷu/}} could occur in Gothic as the result of analogy: for example, the Gothic verb {{transliteration|got|qiman}} {{IPA|/kʷiman/}} (from Proto-Germanic *''kwemaną'' {{IPA|/kʷemɑnɑ̃/}}) had the past participle {{transliteration|got|qumans}} {{IPA|[kʷumans]}}, rather than *{{transliteration|got|kumans}} {{IPA|[kumans]}}, which would be the regular outcome of the Proto-Germanic form *''kumanaz'' {{IPA|/kumɑnɑz/}}.
* The voiced stops {{IPA|[b]}}, {{IPA|[d]}} and {{IPA|[ɡ]}} are noted by the letters {{angbr|b}}, {{angbr|d}} and {{angbr|g}}. They occurred after a nasal (as in other Germanic languages) and apparently also after other consonants:{{sfn|Miller|2019|p=26}} {{transliteration|got|arbi}} {{IPA|[arbi]}} {{gloss|inheritance}}, {{transliteration|got|huzd}} {{IPA|[huzd]}} {{gloss|treasure}}. (This is inferred from their behavior at the end of a word, where they do not change into voiceless fricatives, unlike when they occur after a vowel.) It is generally assumed that ''b d g'' were also pronounced as stops in word-initial position{{sfn|Snædal|2011|pp=147-148}} or when geminate.{{sfn|Miller|2019|p=26}} However, {{harvcoltxt|Bennett|1980|pp=3–4}} gives word-initial ''b d'' as stops, but word-initial ''g'' as a voiced fricative, and assumes geminate ''gg(w)'' had merged with the identically spelled {{IPA|[ŋɡ(ʷ)]}}. Geminate ''bb, dd, gg'' did not occur in native Gothic words outside of the sequences ''ggw'' and ''ddj'', but they could be found in borrowed words (such as {{transliteration|got|Þaddaiu}} {{gloss|Thaddaeus}} or {{transliteration|got|sabbato}} {{gloss|sabbath}}{{sfn|Miller|2019|p=29}}).
* There was probably also a [[voiced velar plosive|voiced labiovelar stop]], {{IPA|[ɡʷ]}}, which was written with the digraph {{angbr|gw}}. It occurred after a nasal, e.g. {{transliteration|got|saggws}} {{IPA|[saŋɡʷs]}} {{gloss|song}}, or long as a regular outcome of Germanic *''ww'': {{transliteration|got|triggws}} {{IPA|[triɡʷːs]}} {{gloss|faithful}} (English ''true'', German ''treu'', Icelandic ''tryggur''). The existence of a long {{IPA|[ɡʷː]}} separate from {{IPA|[ŋɡʷ]}}, however, is not universally accepted.{{sfn|Snædal|2011}}
* The letters {{angbr|ddj}} represented the regular outcome of Germanic *''jj''. Its pronunciation has been variously proposed to be {{IPA|[ɟj]}},{{sfn|Collinge|1985|p=93}} {{IPA|[dːj]}} or {{IPA|[dj]}}.{{sfn|Snædal|2011}}

====Fricatives====
* {{IPA|/s/}} and {{IPA|/z/}} are usually written {{angbr|s}} and {{angbr|z}}. The latter corresponds to Germanic *''z'' (which has [[Rhotacism|rhotacised]] ''r'' or silent in the other Germanic languages); at the end of a word, it is regularly devoiced to ''s'': e.g. {{transliteration|got|saíhs}} {{IPA|[sɛhs]}} {{gloss|six}}, {{transliteration|got|máiza}} {{IPA|[mɛːza]}} {{gloss|greater}} (English ''more'', Dutch ''meer'', German ''mehr'', Icelandic ''meira'') versus {{transliteration|got|máis}} {{IPA|[mɛːs]}} {{gloss|more, rather}}.
* {{IPA|/ɸ/}} and {{IPA|/θ/}}, written {{angbr|f}} and {{angbr|þ}}, are voiceless bilabial and voiceless dental fricatives respectively. It is likely that the relatively unstable sound {{IPA|/ɸ/}} became {{IPA|/f/}}. The cluster {{IPA|/ɸl/}} became {{IPA|/θl/}} in some words but not others: {{transliteration|got|þlauhs}} {{gloss|flight}} from Germanic *''flugiz''; {{transliteration|got|þliuhan}} {{gloss|flee}} from Germanic *''fleuhaną'' (but see {{transliteration|got|flōdus}} {{gloss|river}}, {{transliteration|got|flahta}} {{gloss|braid}}). This sound change is unique among Germanic languages.{{sfn|Davis|Iverson|1994}}
* {{IPA|[β]}}, {{IPA|[ð]}} and {{IPA|[ɣ]}} are [[allophones]] of {{IPA|/b/}}, {{IPA|/d/}} and {{IPA|/ɡ/}} respectively, and are not distinguished from them in writing. The voiced fricative allophones were used when {{IPA|/b, d, ɡ/}} came between vowels, as in {{transliteration|got|haban}} {{IPA|[haβan]}} {{gloss|to have}}, {{transliteration|got|þiuda}} {{IPA|[θiu̯ða]}} {{gloss|people}}, {{transliteration|got|áugo}} {{IPA|[ɔːɣoː]}} {{gloss|eye}}. When preceded by a vowel and followed by a voiceless consonant or by the end of a word, {{IPA|/b, d/}} were devoiced to {{IPA|[ɸ, θ]}} and spelled as {{angbr|f}}, {{angbr|þ}}: e.g. {{transliteration|got|hláifs}} {{IPA|[hlɛːɸs]}} {{gloss|loaf}} but genitive {{transliteration|got|hláibis}} {{IPA|[hlɛːβis]}} {{gloss|of a loaf}}; {{transliteration|got|gif}} {{IPA|[ɡiɸ]}} {{gloss|give (imperative)}} but infinitive {{transliteration|got|giban}} {{IPA|[ɡiβan]}} {{gloss|to give}}; {{transliteration|got|miþ}} {{IPA|[miθ]}} {{gloss|with}} ([[Old English language|Old English]] ''mid'', [[Old Norse]] ''með''). The velar consonant {{IPA|/ɡ/}} was probably also phonetically devoiced in the same position, becoming the voiceless velar fricative {{IPA|[x]}}, but this is less certain; it remained spelled as ''g'' and apparently did not merge with any other phoneme. It is possible that {{IPA|[β]}} developed phonetically to labiodental {{IPA|[v]}}.
* {{IPA|/h/}} (from Proto-Germanic *''h'' [x]) is written as {{angbr|h}}: {{transliteration|got|haban}} {{gloss|to have}}. It could occur in the coda of syllables (e.g. {{transliteration|got|jah}} {{IPA|/jah/}} {{gloss|and}} (Dutch, German, Scandinavian ''ja'' {{gloss|yes}}) and unlike {{IPA|/ɸ/}} and {{IPA|/θ/}}, it did not merge at the end of a word or before a voiceless consonant with its etymologically paired voiced consonant: {{IPA|/ɡ/}} remained written as {{angbr|g}}, e.g. {{transliteration|got|dags}} {{IPA|/dags/}} {{gloss|day}} (German ''Tag''). There are conflicting interpretations of what this data means in terms of phonetics. Some linguists interpret it as a sign that {{IPA|[ɣ]}} failed to be devoiced in this context, but given that the other voiced fricatives were subject to devoicing in this position, {{harvtxt|Howell|1991}} argues it is more likely that {{IPA|/dags/}} was pronounced with devoicing as {{IPA|[daxs]}} and coda {{IPA|/h/}} was pronounced as something other than a voiceless velar fricative. Two phonetic values that have been proposed for syllable-final {{IPA|/h/}} are uvular {{IPA|[χ]}} and glottal {{IPA|[h]}}.{{sfn|Howell|1991|pp=90–91}}
* In some borrowed Greek words there is a special letter {{angbr|x}}, which represents the Greek letter {{lang|grc|χ}} ({{transliteration|grc|ch}}): {{transliteration|got|Xristus}} {{IPA|[xristus]}} {{gloss|Christ}} (Gk. ''{{lang|grc|Χριστός}}'').
* {{angbr|ƕ}} (also transcribed ''hw'') is the labiovelar equivalent of {{IPA|/h/}}, derived from Proto-Indo-European *''kʷ''. It was probably pronounced {{IPA|[ʍ]}} (a voiceless {{IPA|[w]}}), as ''wh'' is pronounced in certain dialects of English and in Scots: {{transliteration|got|ƕan}} {{IPA|/ʍan/}} {{gloss|when}}, {{transliteration|got|ƕar}} {{IPA|/ʍar/}} {{gloss|where}}, {{transliteration|got|ƕeits}} {{IPA|[ʍiːts]}} {{gloss|white}}.

====Sonorants====
* Gothic has three nasal consonants, {{IPA|[m, n, ŋ]}}. The first two are phonemes, {{IPA|/m/}} and {{IPA|/n/}}; the third, {{IPA|[ŋ]}}, is an allophone found only in [[complementary distribution]] with the other two. 
** The bilabial nasal {{IPA|/m/}}, transcribed {{angbr|m}}, can be found in any position in a syllable: e.g. {{transliteration|got|guma}} {{gloss|man}}, {{transliteration|got|bagms}} {{gloss|tree}}.{{sfn|Braune|1883|p=21}}
** The coronal nasal {{IPA|/n/}}, transcribed {{angbr|n}}, can be found in any position in a syllable. It [[assimilation (linguistics)|assimilates]] to the [[place of articulation]] of an immediately following stop consonant: before a [[bilabial consonant]], it becomes {{IPA|[m]}} and before a [[velar consonant|velar stop]], it becomes {{IPA|[ŋ]}}. Thus, clusters like {{IPA|[nb]}} or {{IPA|[nk]}} are not possible.
** The velar nasal {{IPA|[ŋ]}}, transcribed {{angbr|g}}, is not a phoneme and cannot appear freely in Gothic. It occurs only before a velar stop as the result of nasal place assimilation, and so is in [[complementary distribution]] with {{IPA|/n/}} and {{IPA|/m/}}. Following Greek conventions, it is normally written as ''g'' (sometimes ''n''): {{transliteration|got|þagkjan}} {{IPA|[θaŋkjan]}} {{gloss|to think}}, {{transliteration|got|sigqan}} {{IPA|[siŋkʷan]}} {{gloss|to sink}} ~ {{transliteration|got|þankeiþ}} {{IPA|[θaŋkiːθ]}} {{gloss|thinks}}. The cluster ''ggw'' may have denoted two different spoken sounds {{IPA|[ŋɡʷ]}} and {{IPA|[ɡʷː]}} (see above).
* {{IPA|/w/}} is transliterated as {{angbr|w}} before a vowel: {{transliteration|got|weis}} {{IPA|[wiːs]}} {{gloss|we}}, {{transliteration|got|twái}} {{IPA|[twɛː]}} {{gloss|two}} (German {{Lang|de|zwei}}).
* {{IPA|/j/}} is written as {{angbr|j}}: {{transliteration|got|jer}} {{IPA|[jeːr]}} {{gloss|year}}, {{transliteration|got|sakjo}} {{IPA|[sakjoː]}} {{gloss|strife}}.
* {{IPA|/l/}} and {{IPA|/r/}} occur as in other European languages: {{transliteration|got|laggs}} (possibly {{IPA|[laŋɡs]}}, {{IPA|[laŋks]}} or {{IPA|[laŋɡz]}}) {{gloss|long}}, {{transliteration|got|mel}} {{IPA|[meːl]}} {{gloss|hour}} (English ''meal'', Dutch {{Lang|nl|maal}}, German {{Lang|de|Mahl}}, Icelandic {{Lang|is|mál}}). The exact pronunciation of {{IPA|/r/}} is unknown, but it is usually assumed to be a [[Trill consonant|trill]] {{IPA|[r]}} or a [[Flap consonant|flap]] {{IPA|[ɾ]}}): {{transliteration|got|raíhts}} {{IPA|/rɛhts/}} {{gloss|right}}, {{transliteration|got|afar}} {{IPA|[afar]}} {{gloss|after}}.
* {{IPA|/l/}}, {{IPA|/m/}}, {{IPA|/n/}} and {{IPA|/r/}} may occur either between two other consonants of lower sonority or word-finally after a consonant of lower sonority. It is probable that the sounds are pronounced partly or completely as [[syllabic consonant]]s in such circumstances (as in English "bottle" or "bottom"): {{transliteration|got|tagl}} {{IPA|[taɣl̩]}} or {{IPA|[taɣl]}} {{gloss|hair}} (English ''tail'', Icelandic {{Lang|is|tagl}}), {{transliteration|got|máiþms}} {{IPA|[mɛːθm̩s]}} or {{IPA|[mɛːθms]}} {{gloss|gift}}, {{transliteration|got|táikns}} {{IPA|[tɛːkn̩s]}} or {{IPA|[tɛːkns]}} {{gloss|sign}} (English ''token'', Dutch {{Lang|nl|teken}}, German {{Lang|de|Zeichen}}, Icelandic {{Lang|is|tákn}}) and {{transliteration|got|tagr}} {{IPA|[taɣr̩]}} or {{IPA|[taɣr]}} {{gloss|tear (as in crying)}}.

===Accentuation and intonation===
Accentuation in Gothic can be reconstructed through phonetic comparison, [[Grimm's law]], and [[Verner's law]]. Gothic used a [[stress accent]] rather than the [[pitch accent]] of [[Proto-Indo-European language|Proto-Indo-European]]. This is indicated by the shortening of long vowels {{IPA|[eː]}} and {{IPA|[oː]}} to [a] and the loss of short vowels {{IPA|[a]}} and {{IPA|[i]}} in unstressed final syllables.

Just as in other Germanic languages, the free moving [[Proto-Indo-European accent]] was replaced with one fixed on the first syllable of simple words. Accents do not shift when words are inflected. In most compound words, the location of the stress depends on the type of compound:

* In compounds in which the second word is a ''noun'', the accent is on the first syllable of the first word of the compound.
* In compounds in which the second word is a ''verb'', the accent falls on the first syllable of the verbal component. Elements prefixed to verbs are otherwise unstressed except in the context of separable words (words that can be broken in two parts and separated in regular usage such as [[separable verb]]s in German and Dutch). In those cases, the prefix is stressed.{{citation needed|date=June 2025}}<!--Applies to the last sentence. In Krause and Slocum's introduction to Gothic, it is explicitly stated that the separable prefix only has secondary stress, no exceptions.-->

For example, with comparable words from modern Germanic languages:
* Non-compound words: {{transliteration|got|marka}} {{IPA|[ˈmarka]}} {{gloss|border, borderlands}} (English ''march'', Dutch ''mark''); {{transliteration|got|aftra}} {{IPA|[ˈaɸtra]}} {{gloss|after}}; {{transliteration|got|bidjan}} {{IPA|[ˈbiðjan]}} {{gloss|pray}} (Dutch {{Lang|nl|bidden}}, German {{Lang|de|bitten}}, Icelandic {{Lang|is|biðja}}, English ''bid'').
* Compound words:
** Noun first element: {{transliteration|got|guda-láus}} {{IPA|[ˈɡuðalɔːs]}} {{gloss|godless}}.
** Verb second element: {{transliteration|got|ga-láubjan}} {{IPA|[ɡaˈlɔːβjan]}} {{gloss|believe}} (Dutch {{Lang|nl|geloven}}, German {{Lang|de|glauben}} < [[Old High German]] ''g(i)louben'' by [[syncope (phonetics)|syncope]] of the unaccented ''i'').

==Grammar==

===Morphology===

====Nouns and adjectives====
{{Main|Gothic declension}}
Gothic preserves many archaic Indo-European features that are not always present in modern Germanic languages, in particular the rich Indo-European [[declension]] system. Gothic had [[nominative]], [[accusative]], [[genitive]] and [[dative case]]s, as well as vestiges of a [[vocative case]] that was sometimes identical to the nominative and sometimes to the accusative. The three [[grammatical gender|genders]] of Indo-European were all present. Nouns and adjectives were inflected according to one of two [[grammatical number]]s: the singular and the plural.

Nouns can be divided into numerous declensions according to the form of the stem: ''a'', ''ō'', ''i'', ''u'', ''an'', ''ōn'', ''ein'', ''r'', etc. Adjectives have two variants, ''indefinite'' and ''definite'' (sometimes ''indeterminate'' and ''determinate''), with definite adjectives normally used in combination with the definite [[determiner (linguistics)|determiner]]s (such as the [[definite article]] {{transliteration|got|sa/þata/sō}}) while indefinite adjectives are used in other circumstances.{{sfn|Ratkus|2018a}}{{sfn|Ratkus|2018b}} Indefinite adjectives generally use a combination of ''a''-stem and ''ō''-stem endings, and definite adjectives use a combination of ''an''-stem and ''ōn''-stem endings. The concept of "strong" and "weak" declensions that is prevalent in the grammar of many other [[Germanic languages]] is less significant in Gothic because of its conservative nature: the so-called "weak" declensions (those ending in ''n'') are, in fact, no weaker in Gothic (in terms of having fewer endings) than the "strong" declensions (those ending in a vowel), and the "strong" declensions do not form a coherent class that can be clearly distinguished from the "weak" declensions.

Although descriptive adjectives in Gothic (as well as superlatives ending in {{transliteration|got|-ist, -ost}}) and the [[past participle]] may take both definite and indefinite forms, some adjectival words are restricted to one variant. Some pronouns take only definite forms: for example, {{transliteration|got|sama}} {{gloss|same}}, adjectives like {{transliteration|got|unƕeila}} ('constantly', from the root {{transliteration|got|ƕeila}}, 'time'; compare to the English "while"), comparative adjective and [[present participle]]s. Others, such as {{transliteration|got|áins}} {{gloss|some}}, take only the indefinite forms.

